<ToolLayout title="API Gateway Patterns Reference" description="Common API gateway patterns — aggregation, routing, offloading, BFF, and circuit breaking.">
  <GatewayPatternsReference client:load slot="tool" />

  <h2>Why gateways exist beyond simple routing</h2>
  <p>As a system grows into multiple backend services, client applications shouldn't need to know about that internal complexity — a gateway centralizes cross-cutting concerns (auth, rate limiting, aggregation) so individual services can stay focused on their core responsibility.</p>

  <h2>How to use this reference</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>Identify which cross-cutting concern you're trying to solve (auth, aggregation, routing, resilience).</li>
    <li>Match it to the corresponding gateway pattern.</li>
  </ol>

  <h2>Common mistakes</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Turning the gateway into a monolith itself</strong> by putting business logic into it, rather than keeping it focused on cross-cutting infrastructure concerns.</li>
    <li><strong>Not implementing circuit breaking</strong>, allowing one failing backend service to cascade failures across the whole system through the gateway.</li>
    <li><strong>Using one generic gateway for very different client types</strong> (web vs. mobile) when a Backend for Frontend pattern would serve each better.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Use cases</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Designing an API gateway layer for a microservices architecture</li>
    <li>Deciding whether to implement a Backend for Frontend for a new mobile app</li>
    <li>Adding resilience patterns to prevent cascading failures across services</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>FAQ</h2>
  <p><strong>Do I need a gateway for a small application?</strong> Not necessarily — gateway patterns earn their complexity mainly once you have multiple backend services or need to support multiple client types with different needs.</p>
</ToolLayout>
